<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Braniewo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Braunsberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Braniewo, German Braunsberg, is a town in Warmia in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a>. Founded in the thirteenth century in the lands of the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, it became a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> and a centre of trade. After 1466 it passed with Warmia to Poland, and from 1565 it housed an important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/braniewo.jpg" alt="Braniewo" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Archetyp · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Braniewo_-_bazylika_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Braniewo lies on the Pasłęka River about 5 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula-lagoon">Vistula Lagoon</a>, some 35 km north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbląg</a> and 55 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a>. The Polish border with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaliningrad-oblast">Kaliningrad Oblast</a> is 6 km to the north. The German geographer Johann Friedrich Goldbeck derived the older name Brunsberg from Bruno von Schauenburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, who accompanied King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a> in 1254 and 1267. Another suggestion, Brusebergue, camp of the Prussians, is not documented.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1243 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> gave the settlement and the surrounding region of Warmia to the newly created Bishopric of Warmia, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> built his cathedral there. The town received privileges on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> in 1254, was destroyed and depopulated in 1261 during the second Prussian Uprising, and was rebuilt in a new place in 1273 with colonists from Lübeck. Bishop Heinrich Fleming (1278–1300) moved the chapter to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frombork">Frauenburg</a>, now Frombork. A Franciscan abbey followed in 1296 and a new town in 1342. As a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a> until 1608 the town traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>.</p><p>In 1440 it was a founding member of the Prussian Confederation against Teutonic rule, and in 1454 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> incorporated the territory into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> renounced their claims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn">Second Peace of Thorn</a> in 1466. After 1525 many residents turned Lutheran, but in 1526 the town was returned to Prince-Bishop Mauritius Ferber and Warmia stayed predominantly Catholic. Prince-Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaus-hosius">Stanislaus Hosius</a> brought in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> and founded the Collegium Hosianum, and a priestly seminary was added in 1564. Among its students were Andrew Bobola and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poet Maciej Kazimierz Sarbiewski. The Swedes looted its library, which held works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolaus-copernicus">Nicolaus Copernicus</a>, in 1626.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> annexed Braunsberg in 1772 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> and made it part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> college became the State Academy of Braunsberg in 1912, and before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the town had more than 21,000 inhabitants. Soviet troops captured it on 20 March 1945; about 80 percent of the town was destroyed, and it was handed to Polish authorities on 7 July. Polish settlers, many from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, repopulated it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Braniewo is the capital of Braniewo County in the Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship, with 16,907 inhabitants in 2021, and the second largest town in Warmia after Olsztyn. The Gothic St Catherine Church, built in 1346 and destroyed in 1945, was rebuilt after 1979 and declared a Basilica Minor in 2001. Local brewing dates back to the 16th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Braniewo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Danzig, Elbing, Thorn, Kulm, Königsberg and <b>Braunsberg</b> belonged to the Hanseatic League, and the Grand Master was unusual among princes in being treated as a member.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
